= The <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span> breaks with the coalition

<img src="img/Kollwitz_Arbeitslosigkeit.jpg" style="display:block; width:100%; max-width:500px; height:auto; margin:0 auto 1em auto; object-fit:cover; object-position:top;">

<hr>

Originally, the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD's**</span> presence in government had been tolerated due to the need for their support for the Young Plan. As such, with the recent ratification of the Young Plan, President <span style="color: #808080;">Hindenburg</span> now sees no reason for the continued presence of the <span style="color: #c00000;">socialists</span> in government. In a letter written to Chancellor [+ chancellor +], he has outlined his political agenda as well as the dire need to address the mounting deficit in the national budget.  Following a sharp rise in unemployment after Black Thursday, the unemployment insurance fund has been overstretched and is on the verge of insolvency. To ensure that the unemployed still receive their welfare cheques, we worked out a compromise with the other centrist parties which entailed a loan from the government of 150 million marks while the premiums paid by both parties remain the same. Securing near-unanimous support from the cabinet, it had also received the support from the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP’s**</span> right wing. However, the <span style="color: #c00000;">**ADGB**</span> and the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD's**</span> intransigent left wing have rejected our compromise and pressured the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD's**</span> national leadership to reject this offer and leave the government.


-@expelled

@expelled
title: The <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span> shall leave the coalition. 

With the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD's**</span> exit, the current government no longer holds a majority in the Reichstag and Chancellor [+ chancellor +] has submitted his resignation.Therefore, President <span style="color: #808080;">Hindenburg</span> has entrusted Heinrich Bruning, head of our Reichstag Delegation, with the mandate to broker a cabinet, with his Christian Nationalist ideals and commitment to fiscal discipline making him an acceptable candidate for the Chancellorship in this time of crisis. Now, all we can do is wait and see if he a secure a working parlimentary majority.



